From 74db1ef162e395865054b79dd281e7601450e013 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 00:22:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer, windowing/gbm: a base layer that is no picture
 is rebuilt whatever the setting says

A Dolby Vision stream whose base layer is no picture by itself (profile 5, and AV1 profile 10
compatible with nothing: BaseLayerIsNoPicture()) has no other picture to show: played as it is,
which is how Kodi plays it, its colours are wrong (IPTPQc2 shown as YCbCr). With "Dolby Vision as
HDR10 when needed" (videoscreen.hadesdvashdr10) off, ConversionSettingOf() gave NEVER, and
ConversionEligible() returned false on NEVER before it looked at the profile: on an output that
takes HDR10 and no native Dolby Vision, such a stream went out raw. That is what BigRedJim saw on a
Samsung S90H (issue #2, 2026-10-21: green and purple until he turned that setting on), and what the
answer to him promised for the next version: converted whenever native output is not possible,
with no setting needed.

The setting off is now its own value, NO_PICTURE_ONLY: only such a stream is rebuilt, and nothing
for HDR10+ (ConversionEligibleForHDR10Plus() leaves out NEVER and NO_PICTURE_ONLY). NEVER keeps its
meaning, which ConversionSettingFor() gives it: the user chose Kodi's own output for the video, or
native output measured. The setting now governs only the streams whose base layer is a picture by
itself (profiles 7 and 8 and the others): the enhancement layer, Dolby Vision sent as HDR10+, and
"map to the display" rebuilding every stream need it on, as before.

Two things follow from it:
- Native output measured (OutputChoice::MEASURED) is a choice for a base layer that is HDR10 only
  (MeasuredInPlaceOfOwn(), OutputChoiceOffered()); asked of a stream of another kind (the choice
  was made for another video stream of the same file), it gave NEVER here while the native side
  already treated it as automatic. ConversionTargetOf() now lets the settings decide for it, so a
  profile 5 stream is not left raw that way either. An SDR output already mapped such a stream.
- The HDR10+ setting is under "Dolby Vision as HDR10 when needed" and greyed out while that is off,
  but DVPlusPossible() and DVConversionHDR10Plus() read it on its own. A profile 5 stream rebuilt
  with the setting off would have gone out as HDR10+ by a value that the settings window shows as
  not in effect. Both now ask the two (Hdr10PlusSettingOn()). The same holds for a video for which
  "Dolby Vision as HDR10" is chosen in the player while the setting is off: plain HDR10, as the
  window shows. The mapping to the display is no child of that setting and applies to whatever is
  rebuilt, as before.

Nothing changes where HDR10 cannot go out: on an output that takes no HDR10 such a stream is
rebuilt and mapped to SDR (SdrConversionEligible(), whatever the settings say, as before), and
where neither can be done here (no OpenGL ES 3, decoding in software, interlaced or stereoscopic
video, the rebuilding failed in this video, or Kodi's own output chosen) it plays as Kodi plays it.

The help of the setting (core #39990, Estuary #31697 in en_gb, es_es, es_ar and es_mx) says it, and
that of the mapping (core #39833, Estuary #31773) says that a profile 5 video is mapped with the
setting off too. kodi.log says it of the stream, without debugging: "decoded for output as HDR10
where it is not sent natively (the base layer is no picture by itself: rebuilt whatever ...)", and,
where it is not rebuilt, "(the base layer is no picture by itself, and it cannot be rebuilt here:
its colours are wrong)".

Host test (p5always_test, against DVPlaybackPolicy.h): every combination of the settings, the
output, the choice, profile and compatibility, the layer, software, interlaced, stereoscopic, the
tone mapping method and HDR10+ (215 040) against a model of the rule, and 23 cases by name. With the
header before, 7 of the 25 checks fail; with this one, none. Of the combinations, 1 024 change from
the rule before: 384 with the setting off and a base layer that is no picture, 640 with native
measured asked of a base layer that is not HDR10, none else. The projector test's three checks of
"off: NEVER" encode the rule before and are changed in its copy.
